WEBVTT

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:16.600
Heave!

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.720
Two, six.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.080
Heave!

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:23.480
We're back at the very edge of our isles.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:26.320
But now we're on a whole new kind of adventure.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:34.840
A unique Great Guide to our coast.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:42.160
But this is a guide beyond anything you'll find in your average tourist brochure.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:49.240
A guide crammed with local knowledge, amazing discoveries and stunning secret spots.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:01:00.040
Coast and her expert crew have spent over ten years navigating this ever-changing natural wonder.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:09.640
And now we're bringing it all together, and more, to give you the ultimate guide to our coast.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.560
We've selected eight stretches of British coast.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:22.680
North, south, east, west and some of the best bits in between.

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:32.560
Each week, we'll be taking to the sea in a remarkable array of boats and ships.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.080
We'll have a completely fresh perspective on the coast.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.920
We'll seek out charismatic characters...

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.960
Andy, fancy seeing you here!

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.760
..Momentous events...

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:46.680
This is Britain's most deadly shoreline.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:49.440
..Secret spots... and surprising stories.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.040
There's no denying that there's a charge to be had from holding something like this.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:02:02.200
A brand-new view of our coast, with all the inside info you need to enjoy these shorelines like a local.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.400
Haul away, Seamus, haul away!

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:11.400
This time, I'm heading for the south coast.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.520
This is Coast.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.240
The Great Guide.

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:55.040
Welcome to a very select Great Guide.

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:06.920
Shores awash with eye-wateringly expensive real estate.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:12.920
Vessels that redefine luxury.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:23.600
A coast showcasing 200 million years of the Earth's history... ..where dinosaurs roamed and now yachts fly.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:32.880
Here, cutting-edge engineering rubs chic shoulders with vintage craft.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.760
It's our glamour coast.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.640
The sunshine coast.

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:41.720
I'm at the heart of Britain's south coast.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:47.560
Our experts have sought out the sights and stories that make these shores so special.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.080
Spectacular rocks...

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.920
It's fragile, it's unpredictable and every time you pull on a hold, your heart's in your mouth.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.760
..Some of the world's most challenging waters...

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.480
We've lost rudder!

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:03.440
Clear off!

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:06.760
..And simply stunning sea life.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:07.840
It looks sort of alien.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.520
Really, really unusual shape, with those big eyes.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:22.240
To get the inside scoop on these shores, I'll be hopping on and off a variety of boats... ..as I voyage along this spectacular coast.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:33.360
The weather might be a bit gloomy today, I'll grant you that, but on a vessel like this, a beautiful, vintage craft, how can you have anything but fun?

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:46.160
I'll embark from the maritime heart of Portsmouth, I'll take in a cruise around the Solent before powering to Poole.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:52.200
On my voyage, I'll compile our Great Guide from a wider canvass of stories, along a stretch bookended by two pebble beaches.

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:59.440
Brighton in the east, and Chesil in the west.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:05.760
Portsmouth, where my journey begins, is an obvious choice for our guide.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:11.000
A city with a thousand years of proud seafaring tradition.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:18.000
Historic home of the Royal Navy, and some of the world's finest ships.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:34.280
I would say, as a maritime nation founded on naval supremacy, if there's an image, an icon of Britishness, it's not a cathedral, it's not the Palace of Westminster or London Bridge, it's HMS Victory.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:38.520
Nelson's flagship led Britain to triumph at the Battle of Trafalgar.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:44.840
Launched in 1765, she's the oldest vessel in commission in the world.

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.440
She sits here in prestigious company.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:59.600
Inside this futuristic building sits Mary Rose, pride of Henry VIII's fleet.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:02.200
And alongside, an iron trailblazer.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:08.320
This is HMS Warrior, the first iron-hulled, ironclad battleship.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:29.720
But she's still got the elegant lines of the wooden ships that went before her, and I think there is something about vessels like this, built with the hands of men, that means you can see and feel a personality about them that the modern 21st-century battleships just don't have.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:34.600
Ships that built Britain... pioneered and now preserved here.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.320
What made Portsmouth a naval hub?

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.040
Ten years ago, Mark Horton found out.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:52.960
My guide, Roy Rolf, started off by explaining how the geography here works to make this place an ideal port.

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:58.560
Right in the entrance now, you can see it's very narrow.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.040
Presumably defensible, because it's so narrow?

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:02.680
That's right, yes.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:15.000
We're now actually out into the entrance channel to Portsmouth Harbour, and at the shore of the Isle of Wight.

00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:15.160
The Isle of Wight's one of the main reasons why this is such a good harbour.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.160
So it acts as a sort of protection against...

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:22.280
Despite the fact we can see the wind coming in, it's quite sheltered.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:22.600
And it also means that the harbour is always usable.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:32.440
You don't get the sort of weather that you get at Dover sometimes, by the breakwater, where it's something of a lottery to get in and out in really bad weather.

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:43.720
Looking at the steel warships sitting here today, you could be forgiven for thinking the golden age of sail is over.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:43.840
But...

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:49.120
Look closely, and you'll see that the days of rope and canvas have never left Portsmouth.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:08:00.840
It's just, today, they've been completely reinvented, in a whole new battle for supremacy at sea.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:14.960
Going into our guide is the newest addition to Portsmouth's portfolio of vessels, and we've got exclusive access to this cutting-edge prototype... ..designed for four-time Olympic champion, and our most successful competitive sailor, Sir Ben Ainslie.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.080
His latest challenge?

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:30.160
Create a craft and a team to win the 2017 America's Cup.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:49.400
This, the AC45, has already put them in pole position and we're trying our best to keep up with the AC45, which is one of the test vessels that the crew use to go through their paces, so that they'll know what it's like aboard the kind of vessel that they'll use to compete in the America's Cup next year.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:09:02.200
But the really striking thing at the moment is, we're using big outboard engines to try to keep up with this thing, and it's just employing wind power.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:05.440
Sitting up there, it's like a cross between top-end design and some kind of water beetle!

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.720
It almost looks alive over there.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:17.240
Ben's flying yachtsmen have one goal, to win the oldest trophy in international sport.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:24.000
The first America's Cup race actually took place around the Isle of Wight in 1851.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:29.800
A New York schooner called America triumphed, giving the race its name.

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:36.840
But it's a cup Britain has never won.

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:43.520
At Portsmouth, this top team of engineers, designers and sailors are determined to break that duck.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:51.080
To my untrained eye, it's as though the science of sailing is about to leave the water behind altogether.

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:55.920
This looks more like a cross between surfing and flying.

00:09:55.240 --> 00:10:00.920
The secret to this boat's speed is hydrofoil technology.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:13.120
The foils beneath the boat are shaped to deflect water, pushing her above the waves, minimising drag and maximising speed.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:29.720
I've no chance of catching up with Ben on water, so we're heading back to HQ - Portsmouth Harbour, still ruling the waves when it comes to sailing.

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:33.800
Sir Ben Ainslie.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:34.920
Hi, how are you?

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.280
I'm well.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:37.640
Thanks for coming down.

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:39.760
Picked a good day for it.

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.760
Yeah.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.440
What's it like, flying one of them?

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:47.400
We've had up to about 39 knots, or close to 50mph, similar to the eventual race boat.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:53.120
But the race boat will be much bigger and a lot faster.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.240
So, for a sailing boat, that's just incredible.

00:10:53.480 --> 00:11:00.800
And the exhilaration when you lift up out of the water and you start foiling, or flying, feeling the wind rushing by...

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:07.120
It must be a thrill for you, rather than just doing something that's tried and tested, and the work of long experience, to be on the edge of something new?

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:12.200
You know, the America's Cup is a design race as much as it is a sailing race.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:20.000
So we have, you know, a group of 30 or 40 designers here in the base working away incredibly hard to try and get a jump on the competition.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:28.440
And then we have, you know, a great history in sailing, in Olympic sailing most recently, so if we can harness those together, I think we have a really good shot at winning this.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:31.200
We're under no illusions of the challenge we're up against, but at the same time, we're setting our stall out to win.

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:41.400
If we don't win this time, we're going to keep going until we get the job done.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.880
Portsmouth is where they build world-beating boats.

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:51.080
But ten miles over the water, Cowes makes our Great Guide as THE place to race them.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:12:00.720
Home of international yacht racing, and the world's oldest regatta... ..it also comes with the royal seal of approval.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.840
Holidays here gave Prince Edward a taste for competitive sailing.

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:18.000
As King Edward VII, he became Commodore of the Royal Yacht Squadron, the ancient Cowes Club that hosted the first America's Cup race.

00:12:17.160 --> 00:12:30.800
150 years on, the club still hosts an annual round-island race that pits amateurs against professionals... and Mother Nature.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:39.200
For our guide, Nick Crane joined a crew to find out what makes this race one of the yachting world's biggest challenges.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:42.520
That's the start.

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.480
We're off!

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:49.280
Really good blow, helicopter hovering overhead...

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:49.560
Unbelievable.

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.520
Unbelievable.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:57.880
The start is chaos, every yacht competing for water, wind and tide.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:02.600
It's a case of getting out quick and avoiding collisions.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:09.200
That was my first hands-on proper tack, and I didn't muck it up!

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:14.760
Breaking free of the pack, we've stolen a march on our rivals.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:17.840
Yes!

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:18.280
We're doing well.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:18.680
But obstacles await.

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:21.480
Wave!

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:32.680
Just a drag race, who can sail the fastest, win the cleanest air to get to the forks, and then it becomes a fight up to the finish.

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:41.160
We aren't just battling other boats.

00:13:40.480 --> 00:13:51.160
We're also fighting the full force of wind and tide, straining hard on the rudder to steer true.

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:54.880
We've lost rudder!

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:55.480
The rudder linkage has snapped.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.400
The sails take over steering.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:07.720
We're not just helpless, we're dangerous.

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:11.040
We've lost rudder!

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.240
Clear off!

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:11.840
A missile, guided by the wind.

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:12.560
We need to get that under control.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:16.760
Can we get the main in, please?

00:14:17.360 --> 00:14:22.920
If you've got any control, if you just steer us out of this line of boats.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:31.880
All our efforts blown out of the water by a single mechanical failure.

00:14:30.920 --> 00:14:32.280
Our race is run.

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:48.440
You can take your pick of boats to explore this coast in style, from the high-tech to the vintage.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:54.880
This is Hazel Christie, my 1960s water taxi.

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:00.920
This is our Great Guide to the heart of Britain's south coast.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:07.560
I'm leaving historic Portsmouth and heading along the Solent for Langstone Harbour, but the next story in our guide is 45 miles further east.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:16.160
Brighton is a seaside resort by royal appointment.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:30.640
More than 11 million punters pour in each year to visit the exotic pavilion... ..the Palace Pier and, this year, the world's first vertical 360-degree cable car.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:37.240
But none of these attractions earn the city a place in our guide.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:41.480
Brighton's in our Great Guide as a hotbed of early movie-making.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:45.520
It's where they invented this... the close-up.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:51.000
Over 100 years ago, Brighton was Britain's answer to Hollywood.

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:58.120
Film companies sprang up, building studios on the beach to shoot ambitious adaptations.

00:15:57.320 --> 00:16:08.200
It was Brighton showman George Albert Smith who came up with an innovation which changed movie-making for ever.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:14.160
Eight years ago, I explored what he did.

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:20.120
The very first films were pretty static by modern standards.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:25.320
These early films were known as animated photographs.

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:27.240
They captured events as they unfolded in one continuous, unedited shot.

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:31.200
What Smith did was begin to imagine you could build a film sequence.

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:49.160
Instead of conceiving of a single shot, like the frame, you could move from that and you could look at what I'm seeing now of you, how you're looking at me, and also to the sense in which the sea, the sky, the shingle and then the wider space in which we're in.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:17:00.360
Just as we move our camera to get different shots, Smith did the same thing, except he was the first to think of it.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:08.359
Strange to think this is where the modern movie was created, around 1900.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:20.640
Our Great Guide is exploring the heart of Britain's south coast.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:36.320
On the Jurassic Coast, we'll search for secrets in the rock... ..tell you all you need to know about the Isle of Wight, and at Southampton, sail into a world of utter luxury.

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:44.360
The next stop on my journey is just outside Portsmouth, Langstone Harbour.

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:56.320
Here, on this poshest of coasts, what was once a poor man's food is making a comeback.

00:17:55.480 --> 00:18:01.520
Ostrea edulis - to you and me, the oyster.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:03.960
Oysters are a must-eat in our Great Guide to these shores.

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:11.920
I'm here to find out how this native mollusc has fared in recent years.

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:20.680
It might not look like it, but this was once the biggest native oyster fishery in the whole of Europe.

00:18:19.720 --> 00:18:28.280
People have farmed oysters here since Roman times.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:38.640
In the late '70s, 450 boats were making a living from catching oysters in the Solent.

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:39.840
Today, there are none.

00:18:39.040 --> 00:18:40.360
Hi, Neil, good to meet you.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.600
Do I look the part?

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:41.880
Absolutely.

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:42.120
Good.

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:42.840
Come on board.

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:56.040
Dr Joanne Preston from the University of Portsmouth is spearheading a project to revive a devastated industry that once sold oysters to Rome and was worth millions.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:19:02.400
In the good old days, when the beds were healthy, how many oysters would have been here?

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:05.560
In a season, they're landing around 200 tonnes a year.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:13.440
And that dropped, in a relatively short time, to around 20 tonnes, and down to almost nothing.

00:19:12.720 --> 00:19:22.480
Joanne is taking me to see the heart of the operation, moored in waters where oysters once flourished.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.520
It's not the most glamorous raft you've got, is it?

00:19:23.600 --> 00:19:25.720
Certainly not, no.

00:19:24.880 --> 00:19:26.640
But it does the job?

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:28.520
It's a good workhorse.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.880
Can we go aboard?

00:19:31.320 --> 00:19:32.600
Yeah, please do.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:41.360
The rusty raft is used to suspend cages of young native oysters at varying depths in the tidal currents.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:42.480
This is the protected brood stock.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:53.120
The idea is that these can't be fished, they're less susceptible to disease and these will grow and spawn and then repopulate the seabed population.

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:53.560
So this is a trial that we hope to upscale.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:56.400
What exactly happened to the oysters here?

00:19:55.520 --> 00:20:02.840
We can't pinpoint one main factor, but certainly, overfishing is part of it.

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:03.920
But there's also a lot of other things.

00:20:03.160 --> 00:20:05.440
There's several diseases.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:15.080
The other thing is that, as the population declines, there's an invasive species called American slipper limpet, also known as Crepidula fornicata.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:26.800
And that actually came over from North America into Essex, with oysters, at the turn of the 18th and 19th century, and this has become almost the dominant species on the bottom of the Solent now.

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.520
So if you were on the seabed now, it's those American limpets that you would see?

00:20:29.800 --> 00:20:31.760
Yeah.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:33.560
They spread quite quickly...

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:36.480
Hence "fornicata", I'm guessing?

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:37.400
Yes, hence fornicata!

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:42.520
They grow in stacks and change sex.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:51.640
Yeah, so they are competing for space, for nutrients, for food, so it's a bit of a triple whammy in terms of their impact.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:52.080
What will you do with them now?

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:52.600
What's the drill here?

00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:53.000
The first thing is, do they survive?

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:55.840
So we're counting mortality and we're also seeing if they grow, and by how much.

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:59.240
We take the maximal length, maximal width, and then we take the depth.

00:21:01.240 --> 00:21:02.360
And then we weigh them.

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:08.400
And the most important thing is, are they spawning?

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:09.880
So, are they reproducing?

00:21:09.040 --> 00:21:10.680
How can you tell that?

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:16.640
We put settlement plates just outside the cages, and so, hopefully, any spawn can settle on those.

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:19.760
We're also monitoring the oyster larvae in the water.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:20.320
Why such an effort to bring them back?

00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:25.440
What do the oysters do for an environment?

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:25.760
They're filter feeders.

00:21:24.920 --> 00:21:28.680
And they filter up to five gallons of water an hour.

00:21:27.800 --> 00:21:29.000
Each?

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:29.240
Each.

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:29.840
One oyster.

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:36.560
And so, if you've got them naturally occurring in oyster reefs, then they do an amazing job of cleaning the water.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:41.840
But they also increase the amount of vertebrates associated with them, which means it's beneficial for other organisms.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:46.560
And, actually, it's been known that, if you restore oyster beds, then fishing production increases as well.

00:21:45.920 --> 00:21:49.800
When it comes to them as a food source, do you like oysters?

00:21:48.880 --> 00:21:53.400
I had my first oyster two weeks ago.

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:53.560
Yes.

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:54.480
Your first oyster?

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:55.160
Yeah!

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.600
Wow.

00:21:55.640 --> 00:22:04.600
It was quite early in the morning, and it was the first thing I'd eaten that day and it wasn't seasoned, so I'm reserving judgment at the moment.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:05.960
Right.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:06.160
So would we, in theory, be able to eat one of these?

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:10.280
I wouldn't recommend eating these at the moment, just because the water quality isn't good enough.

00:22:09.440 --> 00:22:14.080
So these oysters are very much a work in progress?

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:18.920
They are a work in progress, yes.

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:26.040
There's another fishy reason why this part of the coast earns its place in the Great Guide.

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:28.160
A natural wonder that washes in at spring.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:30.000
Cuttlefish.

00:22:29.160 --> 00:22:34.200
It's one of the only places in Britain to spot these rarities.

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:39.840
Miranda Krestovnikoff joined them underwater.

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:45.880
They're such exotic-looking creatures, you'd never imagined you'd find something like this right here in British waters.

00:22:46.320 --> 00:22:50.080
It's very big.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.440
A couple of feet long.

00:22:51.200 --> 00:22:55.120
They look sort of alien.

00:22:54.440 --> 00:23:00.920
Really, really unusual shape, with those big eyes and this floating skirt.

00:23:00.080 --> 00:23:03.680
Really odd-looking creatures.

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:11.760
Cuttlefish are in the same family as squid and octopus.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:20.840
Sometimes known as the chameleon of the sea, they can change their body colour and patterning to mesmerise their prey.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:29.280
And off it goes...

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.040
Oh!

00:23:29.080 --> 00:23:30.320
Oh, gosh, what's he got?

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:30.760
Oh, my goodness me!

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:31.320
He's just grabbed a crab!

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:32.720
That's amazing.

00:23:31.760 --> 00:23:39.080
I've never seen that before.

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:52.280
Cuttlefish have a sharp, parrot-like beak and a venomous bite, which will make short work of this crab.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:58.200
As the sea warms in spring, cuttlefish invade these shallow waters to mate and lay their eggs.

00:23:57.400 --> 00:24:05.920
Male cuttlefish dazzle the smaller females with their striped patterns and flowing tentacles.

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:11.720
They mate head-to-head, with tentacles entwined.

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:26.360
After mating, the male cuttlefish guards his female as she deposits her eggs, dyed black with ink to deter predators.

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:40.960
This cuttlefish invasion lasts all summer, but as the water cools, and with their life cycle complete, both males and females die, leaving their bones to be washed up on the beach.

00:24:41.360 --> 00:24:51.800
A decade later, cuttlefish are still congregating on this coast, although their life expectancy is shrinking.

00:24:51.000 --> 00:24:56.720
Scientists are continuing to study these strange critters to find out why.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:25:01.800
This is our Great Guide to the heart of Britain's south coast.

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:15.240
For ten years, our experts have explored every inch of these shores.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:24.720
But if you were on a whistle-stop tour, what are the unmissable sights to say you've seen this coast?

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:26.000
This is our flying visit.

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:33.920
From Brighton to Chesil, 130 miles of stunning sights.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:47.120
You could start your journey at Rottingdean, a tiny town with a big secret in the surf - the remains of an eccentric Victorian railway.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:54.560
It was absolutely enormous, it stood on legs 24 feet high, the deck was 50 feet long.

00:25:53.880 --> 00:26:02.960
On the top, there was a cabin that could carry 30 passengers in comfort with stained-glass windows, chandeliers...

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:15.280
At Selsey Bill, dive deep for a graveyard of World War II shells and the tanks than never fired them.

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:31.200
If you prefer to be on top of the water, head for Hayling Island, one of Britain's best windsurfing spots.

00:26:30.640 --> 00:26:40.680
Portsmouth is the military heart of this coast, and just offshore rests a powerful relic of war.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:48.800
Nick Hewitt explored Nab Tower, built in 1918 as a German U-boat defence.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:53.520
Up close, the Nab Tower is enormous.

00:26:53.720 --> 00:26:55.080
This is just amazing.

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:59.680
I've looked at this for years and years from shore side, but I've never been this close.

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:01.960
Look at the rust on that.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:08.440
Further down the Solent, Southampton, Britain's oldest continually active port.

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:19.360
A proud island surveys the shores here - the Isle of Wight... ..with its famous lighthouse and an exceptionally clean lens.

00:27:18.760 --> 00:27:20.120
How often does the lens get cleaned, then?

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:22.280
Just once a year.

00:27:21.440 --> 00:27:22.960
It's going to take about that long!

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:27.320
I'd hate to be responsible for a smear.

00:27:27.000 --> 00:27:36.160
Past Bournemouth's boltholes and beaches, to reach Studland.

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:40.360
You don't have to take your clothes off, but if you want to, you've got plenty of room.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:50.040
I come across here to Studland, because it's such a fabulous place to be, very relaxing and the views and the scenery here are unbelievable.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:56.680
Enjoying naturism on a beach, I don't think I could think of anything more special than that.

00:27:57.480 --> 00:28:04.080
Today, the naturist beach has been extended by 50 metres to meet growing demand.

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:08.280
Old Harry Rocks mark the end of this bay, and the start of a stretch with out-of-this-world geology.

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:16.640
Kimmeridge.

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:20.920
Lulworth Cove.

00:28:20.560 --> 00:28:23.280
Durdle Door.

00:28:23.600 --> 00:28:26.560
Beyond that are waters that present a navigational nightmare for sailors.

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:33.760
At Portland Bill is a treacherous tidal surge, known as the Portland Race.

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:37.280
The Cresta Run of the English Channel.

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:41.960
Alan's in the wheelhouse.

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:46.600
He's about to cut the engine and we're going to get sucked into the Portland Race.

00:28:45.800 --> 00:28:48.240
I've got my heart in my mouth, I don't mind admitting it.

00:28:47.480 --> 00:28:51.600
Boat's going all over the place, like a cork.

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:53.480
It's exhilarating, but it's also a bit frightening.

00:28:57.920 --> 00:29:10.320
These tidal forces created the 18-mile strip of shingle that marks the end of our flying visit - Chesil Beach.

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:14.640
But why settle for a whistle-stop tour when there's so much more to discover?

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:16.040
Follow us for the bigger picture.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:32.400
I'm on the Solent, a stunning strait over 20 miles that snakes between Portsmouth, Southampton and the Isle of Wight.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:35.560
My next destination is Portsmouth's Trafalgar Wharf.

00:29:36.560 --> 00:29:50.200
But the next story for our guide is on a stretch 60 miles further west, known as the Jurassic Coast, a geologist's dream.

00:29:49.480 --> 00:29:59.640
Across 95 miles, it spans three geological periods, that's 200 million years of Earth's history.

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:09.000
This shoreline is a World Heritage Site, up there with the Great Barrier Reef and the Grand Canyon.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:19.800
It contains three types of rock, starting in the east with Cretaceous rocks from the time dinosaurs roamed the planet.

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:29.680
Further along, and older, fossil-rich Jurassic rocks.

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:33.720
Then, at the western end, the oldest.

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:39.920
Triassic red rocks, 250 million years old.

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:47.920
But it's the Jurassic rocks at Kimmeridge that go into our Great Guide.

00:30:47.040 --> 00:30:52.880
These orange stripes are bands of Kimmeridge shale.

00:30:51.920 --> 00:31:00.680
Alice Roberts joined a geochemical experiment to reveal a secret hidden inside.

00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:02.520
Let's break a bit off.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:03.880
There we go.

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:06.080
Is that a big enough piece?

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:08.960
I don't believe it's going to set on fire.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:12.160
It's a piece of rock.

00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:15.240
I think you'll find it will.

00:31:14.280 --> 00:31:16.920
It's just beginning to catch there.

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:17.400
Yeah.

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.840
You can see there's lots of smoke comes off him.

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:21.480
Oh, it's definitely on fire!

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:23.520
And it stinks!

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:23.960
It stinks.

00:31:23.080 --> 00:31:25.200
It really is bad, yeah, that's right.

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:31.920
You can see the oil coming off it on the surface of the Kimmeridge shale.

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:32.600
That's oil, as we understand oil to be.

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:39.120
Yeah, absolutely, the Kimmeridge shale has actually been the source rock of the majority of the oil in the North Sea.

00:31:38.280 --> 00:31:44.600
When you say the source rock, it's actually the same layer as we've got here?

00:31:43.720 --> 00:31:45.000
It is, absolutely, yeah.

00:31:44.200 --> 00:31:46.480
That's the real magic of Kimmeridge oil shale.

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:48.640
This rock is the reason we have North Sea oil.

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:59.440
The same strata that are visible on the south coast are buried 3.5km deep, under the oil wells off the northeast of Scotland.

00:31:59.240 --> 00:32:06.680
Over millions of years, at the high temperatures and pressures deep under the seabed, the oil shale produces oil.

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:17.520
In place of 10 million years at 100 degrees centigrade, we can do 30 seconds at 500 degrees centigrade and drive some oil off in the test tube.

00:32:16.600 --> 00:32:21.080
Then, if you want to hold that in the tongs...

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:28.240
So you can see, it's not actually burning, it's just driving the oil off, so there you can see all that brown...

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:30.040
It looks like smoke, it's actually just oil being distilled off the rock.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:32.600
And you can see droplets of oil around here on the side.

00:32:32.920 --> 00:32:34.520
The brown stuff?

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.200
Yeah.

00:32:35.400 --> 00:32:36.640
So that's actually oil that's being driven off.

00:32:35.680 --> 00:32:37.240
Wow.

00:32:36.280 --> 00:32:49.240
Above the beach at Kimmeridge is a commercial operation producing 80 barrels of oil a day.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:51.280
Prospectors continue to scour this coastline for the next big fossil-fuel find.

00:32:55.280 --> 00:33:03.440
As for me, I'm searching out the next big thing in the world of boats for our guide.

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:14.800
I'm speeding towards a dockyard in Portsmouth, where some of the world's most expensive super, mega and giga yachts are designed and built.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:21.560
Trafalgar Wharf might seem a rather drab affair, but don't be fooled by appearances.

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:33.440
That might look like a common or garden industrial estate, but in there are companies involved with the construction of some of the finest super-yachts on the globe.

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:37.480
Apparently, Sir Alan Sugar's yacht is in there being refitted, but I can't tell you where.

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:41.800
Inside another hangar, there's something called a glider yacht.

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:47.360
It's as fast as a speedboat, without the bumps.

00:33:46.960 --> 00:33:52.520
It'll get you from St Tropez to Monaco in an hour, but without messing your hair.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.360
Elsewhere, there's a company building some of the tallest freestanding structures in the world.

00:33:53.400 --> 00:33:55.440
They're masts for yachts.

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:59.600
But I can't tell you the name of the yacht, I can't tell you the name of who the yacht is owned by.

00:33:58.720 --> 00:34:05.080
Over there is a company that puts the hush in hush-hush.

00:34:04.240 --> 00:34:15.000
They're the brains behind these enormous masts, a company applying the sort of technology you'd normally find in Formula 1 and aerospace engineering to the super-yacht.

00:34:14.719 --> 00:34:15.920
Clive.

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:17.159
Pleased to meet you.

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:17.639
I've been looking forward to this.

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:24.440
I'm meeting CEO Clive Johnson to find out how and why they make the masts so big.

00:34:23.560 --> 00:34:26.520
Now I'm going to see what it's all about.

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:30.920
Oh, my.

00:34:30.679 --> 00:34:35.880
Gosh, that's the most outlandish object.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:36.800
You know, the scale of it...

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.360
Yeah, it's pretty big.

00:34:37.679 --> 00:34:42.880
This is actually one of three masts for a super-yacht.

00:34:42.760 --> 00:34:44.840
It doesn't look like any mast I've ever seen.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:48.120
If you walked me in here and said, "Guess what this is?"

00:34:47.199 --> 00:34:51.159
Yacht mast is very far down the list.

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:54.719
I can't imagine how anything this size could possibly have anything to do with it.

00:34:55.360 --> 00:35:00.600
There are these billionaires out there that want the luxury toy, to be the first, to be the best, to be the biggest.

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:06.360
And in this case, it happens to be a super-yacht with a radical rig design.

00:35:05.480 --> 00:35:07.400
Is it the biggest you've made?

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:08.120
It's not.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:14.400
We produced a mast last year that was 110 metres, which is the largest mast in the world.

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:15.600
And what exactly is it made of?

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:16.400
This is a composite material, carbon.

00:35:15.560 --> 00:35:17.080
Why?

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:22.120
When I think of a ship's mast, obviously, I think of the tallest tree in the forest.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:25.040
Traditionally, that's right.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:30.120
It was wooden masts, and then we moved onto steel and aluminium.

00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.240
Once you get to the scale of this kind of mast, it's actually very difficult to use those kind of materials.

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:45.440
They're very heavy, and they have problems when you've got loads in different directions so one of the beautiful things about carbon is that you can lay the fibre in the direction of the forces, and that gives you the ability to keep it light.

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:47.160
How do you use a thing like this?

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:53.400
I don't suppose people are going up and down it on ropes, doing anything in the traditional way.

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:53.600
How does it work?

00:35:52.640 --> 00:35:54.920
Three people can sail this boat.

00:35:53.960 --> 00:35:55.360
You push buttons and the sails come out of the slots in five different sets.

00:35:54.680 --> 00:36:01.480
So if the wind gets up, you can de-canvas very quickly and take the top sets of sails down.

00:36:00.800 --> 00:36:02.480
What are the limits?

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:09.000
What, potentially, could you make if there was a billionaire rich enough and ambitious enough?

00:36:08.280 --> 00:36:17.600
Well, I'm sure that somebody will want to beat the 110-metre mast, and there are discussions of 125-metre masts.

00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:17.920
The technology is certainly there.

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:20.440
Gosh, 110, 120...

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:20.960
How tall is that?

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:26.520
If it could sail down the Thames with its masts up, what would it be taller than?

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:26.960
That's taller than Big Ben.

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:41.280
From the days of wooden warships, to the cutting-edge technology of today, Portsmouth's reputation as a global maritime trailblazer continues.

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:49.920
Commissioning your own super-yacht is the preserve of the super-rich and this coast has more than its fair share of millionaires.

00:36:49.960 --> 00:36:57.800
You need to have some serious money to live the high life on this coast.

00:36:57.040 --> 00:37:05.840
My next port of call is Southampton, for a temporary taste of that high life.

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:11.720
But the next story in our guide is further west, on our most exclusive peninsula.

00:37:10.840 --> 00:37:23.640
Sandbanks is Britain's answer to Palm Beach, with the fourth-most expensive land on the planet.

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:31.520
A wee while ago, I couldn't resist a snoop around one of its priciest properties.

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:38.000
In my wildest dreams, I couldn't afford a house like this.

00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:40.480
Right, Neil, this is it.

00:37:40.600 --> 00:37:48.520
Right!

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:56.600
Nearly every room in this house has a view of the sea.

00:37:55.680 --> 00:37:57.000
Even the bathroom.

00:37:56.120 --> 00:38:00.480
You have your own jetty.

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:00.760
You've got two jetties.

00:37:59.840 --> 00:38:00.960
Yeah.

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:03.480
Well, of course, you'd need two.

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:07.840
What with this house be worth if it wasn't on Sandbanks?

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:09.280
Half the price.

00:38:08.520 --> 00:38:10.080
Right.

00:38:09.440 --> 00:38:13.720
And with probably more land.

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:21.440
But who'd want more land, when you can wake up to 10,000 acres of stunning harbour at the bottom of your garden?

00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:27.000
This is what a house like this is really all about, isn't it?

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:28.360
It's access to all of... that.

00:38:27.440 --> 00:38:29.400
It's a different kind of life, isn't it?

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.960
It's a different world.

00:38:31.080 --> 00:38:40.600
It is a beautiful place, and there are people out there prepared to pay the money for this location.

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:42.000
Do you want it?

00:38:41.920 --> 00:38:43.000
Do I want it?

00:38:42.680 --> 00:38:46.640
Nah.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:39:06.600
Today, Sandbanks is worth a whopping £933 million, with house prices reaching £1,300 a square foot - a 100% increase in just eight years.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:24.520
My new journey into some of the south coast's most exclusive enclaves has brought me to Southampton's Ocean Village Marina, home to some seriously high-end ships, tailor-made for maritime millionaires.

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:30.640
And finally, I'm going aboard the sort of vessel where I've always felt I belong!

00:39:29.720 --> 00:39:32.920
Neil, very good to meet you.

00:39:32.000 --> 00:39:33.400
You too.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:34.680
Welcome aboard.

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:46.760
And a first for me - yachtsman David Tydeman is trusting me at the helm of this £5.5 million beauty, super-yacht Reina.

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:51.320
You can go just to the left of the buoy.

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:52.560
That's great.

00:39:51.640 --> 00:39:59.480
A 25-metre bespoke craft, she comes with cutting-edge mod cons, sleek lines and a professional crew.

00:40:00.120 --> 00:40:09.160
But Reina still offers millionaires with wanderlust a hands-on sailing experience.

00:40:08.440 --> 00:40:17.080
There's a different philosophy about a vessel like this, isn't there, and, say, the kind of super-yacht that most people think of when they hear the term.

00:40:16.200 --> 00:40:17.600
The big motor cruisers.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:20.200
The Abramovich-style vessel.

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:21.200
Yes, that sort of super-yacht is, in some ways, a floating hotel.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:25.440
This is a hands-on sailing boat for an owner who knows what he's doing and wants to enjoy sailing.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:36.880
How much of a product of 21st-century design and understanding is this vessel, compared to the traditions of the old wooden-hulled boats?

00:40:35.960 --> 00:40:42.360
The basic philosophy of hull shape - a keel, a mast - those are traditions that haven't changed.

00:40:41.480 --> 00:40:48.840
It's still a sailing yacht, you sail this in the same way that you would a dinghy.

00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:49.040
We've just refined it, we've used modern design, modern materials.

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:55.680
It makes the boat easier to sail and therefore the whole experience becomes more luxurious.

00:40:54.800 --> 00:41:01.880
And so it imposes no limits on a person's ambitions when it comes to the voyage itself?

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:04.600
No, this boat could go anywhere.

00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:08.280
You could sail this to the Falklands, you could sail it to Alaska.

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:09.480
It would be equally home turning up in Monte Carlo.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:13.680
Our destination is not quite Monte Carlo.

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:21.880
To allow me to have a little taste of luxury life at sea, though, we're taking Reina around the Isle of Wight.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:29.320
There's no question that Reina sails magnificently, but it's below deck that really puts the super in super-yacht.

00:41:31.640 --> 00:41:38.240
Goodness, it's like a beautifully appointed home on land.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:39.960
Well, it's a home from home, that's what we like to build.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:44.080
My eyes have just caught all the perfect attention to detail.

00:41:43.240 --> 00:41:46.840
Can I see where my cabin would be?

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:47.480
Yes, let me show you.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:47.640
If I was the master of the ship.

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:48.960
Let me show you the master cabin.

00:41:49.800 --> 00:41:52.400
Nothing feels cramped either.

00:41:51.560 --> 00:41:54.440
There's all this headroom.

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:57.200
Two guest cabins.

00:41:56.400 --> 00:41:57.760
Look at that!

00:41:56.800 --> 00:42:00.880
And then forward into the master cabin.

00:41:59.920 --> 00:42:03.120
It's so big.

00:42:02.280 --> 00:42:04.040
Oh, for goodness' sake!

00:42:03.080 --> 00:42:06.160
Look at that.

00:42:05.320 --> 00:42:06.720
Look at the en-suite as well.

00:42:05.960 --> 00:42:10.680
I seldom see the inside of a hotel this nice.

00:42:09.960 --> 00:42:16.120
You can imagine doing two weeks across the Atlantic in here, can you?

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:24.640
You know, I've done a bit of sailing, but the idea of being in this kind of palatial comfort at the same time just messes with my head.

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:28.360
How many vessels like this are there on the planet?

00:42:27.480 --> 00:42:32.360
There's about 700 sailing yachts been built ever.

00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:34.600
So these are rare birds.

00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:38.760
Yes, there are 20 boats of this size built per year, only, worldwide.

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:39.160
Uh-huh.

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:41.520
And we build five of them in England.

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:42.640
Gosh.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:44.600
So you join an elite club when you take ownership of one of these.

00:42:43.760 --> 00:42:45.440
Yes.

00:42:54.040 --> 00:43:08.400
A yacht like this is designed and equipped to go anywhere on the globe, but I have to say, on a day like today, it feels perfectly at home right here on the Solent.

00:43:07.640 --> 00:43:19.680
We are heading towards England's largest island, the Isle of Wight, a must-see on these shores.

00:43:19.720 --> 00:43:26.920
Enmeshed in the rocks that make this place so distinctive are epic stories.

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:32.640
The Coast experts have explored the secrets of the Isle of Wight's geology from every conceivable angle.

00:43:31.840 --> 00:43:41.560
From the very birth of this island... ..its role in the space race, to the mystery of what makes up its magnificent Needles.

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:51.240
Our Isle of Wight story started with Nick, going back thousands of years to a time when the island wasn't actually an island.

00:43:51.800 --> 00:43:58.560
Can you describe exactly what we'd have seen 10,000 years ago if we'd looked from here towards what is now Dorset?

00:43:57.640 --> 00:44:03.120
Well, we'd have seen a line of white-chalk cliffs and, behind that, you'd have had clifftops covered in primitive grasses.

00:44:03.760 --> 00:44:17.160
And as you walked back away from that, that coastal environment, you'd have walked into ancient woodlands and then slowly down onto the shores of the estuary of the River Solent.

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:17.720
Sounds like a paradise.

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:20.280
Indeed.

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:22.880
So, how did that woodland paradise become an island?

00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:32.240
20,000 years ago, Northern Europe, and most of Britain, was covered with a layer of glacial ice over a mile thick.

00:44:31.280 --> 00:44:37.400
It started to warm up, the ice melted and water levels rose.

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:39.640
But that wasn't the only thing that helped create the Isle of Wight.

00:44:40.000 --> 00:44:48.640
The other process is best illustrated by two men with an inflatable bed.

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:58.440
OK, this is the primitive United Kingdom, we're going to have Scotland at one end, the Isle of Wight on the other end.

00:44:57.520 --> 00:44:59.360
So, this is the north.

00:44:58.600 --> 00:44:59.760
It is.

00:44:58.800 --> 00:45:00.040
It's very malleable, as you can see.

00:44:59.080 --> 00:45:01.520
You're saying the surface of the planet really is this bendy in places?

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:01.840
Yes, geologically speaking.

00:45:00.960 --> 00:45:13.440
Now, 20,000 years ago, Scotland was covered with 2km-thick of ice, enormous amount of weight, and I want you to be that weight, so on you go.

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:17.040
If I bring in the Isle of Wight, put that in place.

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:24.120
Then we wind the clock forward to about 12,000 years ago, the glaciers are melting away off Scotland really rapidly.

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:25.640
So, off you get.

00:45:24.720 --> 00:45:25.880
It's dropped.

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:27.040
It sinks down a bit.

00:45:26.080 --> 00:45:27.440
That is called isostatic rebound.

00:45:26.880 --> 00:45:40.480
But what's happened to the Isle of Wight is, not only have we got sea levels attacking it, sea level rise from all the glacial water going into the sea, but you've got the isostatic rebound happening.

00:45:39.880 --> 00:45:47.760
So the sea is now going to come churning round this particular lump of rock and turn it into the Isle of Wight that we see today.

00:45:48.160 --> 00:45:56.720
It took a few thousand years before the Isle of Wight was totally cut off, as we see it today.

00:45:56.920 --> 00:46:03.360
And this restless traveller is still moving, still evolving.

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:10.760
65 million years ago, tectonic plates on the surface of the planet collided.

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:21.520
This crash created a geological legacy, a chalk ridge that runs the length of the Isle of Wight.

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:31.560
Millions of years later, this curved coastline would become a frontier of scientific discovery... ..the setting for Britain's space programme.

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:39.640
Cut into the chalk was a space base called High Down, where men tested top-secret space rockets.

00:46:38.840 --> 00:46:47.360
For our guide, Alice Roberts met some of the Isle of Wight's original spacemen.

00:46:46.560 --> 00:46:49.320
So, we're on our way up to High Down?

00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:49.600
There were never this many people around when we worked here.

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:53.240
Very restricted, who could come up here at that time.

00:46:52.520 --> 00:47:01.040
Why did they particularly choose this site for the rocket testing?

00:47:00.240 --> 00:47:10.560
Well, it has a natural bowl and we wanted the noise and the exhaust steam to go out into the bay, so that it didn't go back towards the populated areas of the island.

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:20.520
There was already three gun emplacements here, with magazine stores underneath, which only needed a very small amount of modification to make this into a working rocket site.

00:47:19.560 --> 00:47:23.720
Were the rockets actually built on the site here?

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:28.240
No, the actual rocket was put together at East Cowes and came out here for testing.

00:47:27.360 --> 00:47:42.920
And then we would bring it down to one of these gantries and we would go all through the procedure necessary to actually launch the rocket, including lighting the engines, and you would see the motors with their shock diamonds coming out.

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:46.920
So you'd be watching this rocket there, and you'd be seeing the flames coming out of the bottom of it?

00:47:46.040 --> 00:47:47.400
Yes, and big steam out there.

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:48.000
And quite a roar.

00:47:48.200 --> 00:47:49.320
The only thing we didn't do was let it go.

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:56.280
There was a small ball in the bottom of the rocket and there's a claw which grabs hold of it.

00:47:55.440 --> 00:48:01.000
And when we wanted it to go, the claw was open and away it goes.

00:48:00.120 --> 00:48:01.800
But we didn't do that here.

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:11.240
22 rockets developed and tested here on the Isle of Wight were eventually launched into space.

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:15.800
The scientists left the site in the 1970s, but...

00:48:14.920 --> 00:48:22.520
The geology of the Isle of Wight is a magnet.

00:48:21.920 --> 00:48:29.680
In recent years, the chalk has pulled in a different type of scientist, on a mission to solve a mystery.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:36.880
Why have the famous Needles resisted the sea when the surrounding rock crumbled away long ago?

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:42.120
Answering that question for our guide required a head for heights and nerves of steel.

00:48:41.280 --> 00:48:46.800
Just the job for Andy Torbet.

00:48:47.480 --> 00:48:59.640
We need a pure sample of chalk from the summit to work out why this pinnacle has defied the sea for so long.

00:48:58.680 --> 00:49:00.200
That was my next handhold.

00:48:59.360 --> 00:49:03.920
Below!

00:49:03.120 --> 00:49:04.840
It's fragile, it's crumbly, it's unpredictable.

00:49:04.120 --> 00:49:12.040
And every time you pull on a hold or step up, your heart's in your mouth.

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:31.080
The geologists tell me that from the sealine to the top, this rock face represents about a million years, so for every metre I go up, that's about 30,000 years.

00:49:35.520 --> 00:49:42.840
That's a bit easier.

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:44.240
Done it.

00:49:43.520 --> 00:49:44.880
Still in one piece.

00:49:44.080 --> 00:49:55.560
We'll get the rock samples the guys need, get ourselves down, and that'll be mission accomplished.

00:49:55.240 --> 00:50:00.960
Sample in hand, hopefully we can clear up one mystery right now.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:02.840
Why have the Needles lasted so long?

00:50:03.680 --> 00:50:08.760
Is the chalk harder than the surrounding coast?

00:50:07.880 --> 00:50:09.640
We've got a way to find out.

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:14.400
This device will give a number to the hardness of our sample.

00:50:13.440 --> 00:50:14.600
There's the vein.

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:15.160
Here we have a reading.

00:50:14.200 --> 00:50:18.560
This one's 22.

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:20.320
That's quite hard for chalk.

00:50:19.560 --> 00:50:21.840
Yeah, I think we have a flat surface....

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:26.080
Now we need to compare it with the chalk the geologists have brought along from the mainland.

00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:33.200
It's barely reading 10 on here, which is significantly lower than the one from the Needle.

00:50:32.400 --> 00:50:34.720
This is much, much harder.

00:50:34.920 --> 00:50:41.960
So why is the Needles chalk much harder than normal chalk?

00:50:41.360 --> 00:50:49.760
This chalk was moved by Earth forces and it was bent over until it was nearly vertical.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:57.800
And the compression on that chalk made it significantly harder than other samples of chalk that we see around the south of England.

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:08.520
The Isle of Wight, a time capsule comprising millions of years of history.

00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:15.960
No wonder this geological marvel pulls in over two million tourists each year.

00:51:15.600 --> 00:51:22.080
The Isle of Wight isn't the only holiday heavyweight in our Great Guide to Britain's south coast.

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:24.840
Refined resorts dot these shores.

00:51:23.960 --> 00:51:33.120
But 50 miles from the Isle of Wight is a resort that once offered tourists a spectacle like no other.

00:51:32.440 --> 00:51:36.240
It wasn't out there, it was up there.

00:51:37.800 --> 00:51:40.800
Going into our guide is Worthing.

00:51:39.920 --> 00:51:48.120
Above the beach in the 1950s, the race to set a world airspeed record played out.

00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:57.400
In the summer of 1953, 22-year-old pilot Neville Duke triumphed for Britain.

00:51:57.400 --> 00:52:07.040
Brendan Walker followed in his slipstream in one of those early jets, an authentic Hawker Hunter.

00:52:08.320 --> 00:52:12.280
Even 60 years on, this is a truly impressive piece of engineering.

00:52:12.040 --> 00:52:16.920
The speed we're picking up, it's just incredible!

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:25.880
I wish the camera could see just how much I was smiling right now.

00:52:26.800 --> 00:52:29.440
Away we go.

00:52:34.280 --> 00:52:35.400
Ah, this is fantastic!

00:52:34.440 --> 00:52:40.840
Oh, immediately can feel the G-force just pushing me into the chair.

00:52:41.000 --> 00:52:44.760
And we're so close to the ground!

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:55.360
Pilots like Duke coped with immense pressures, making split-second decisions that not only set records, but could cost them their lives.

00:52:55.760 --> 00:53:00.480
Picking up some speed now, putting full power on.

00:52:59.560 --> 00:53:00.720
You can hear that, it's fantastic!

00:52:59.760 --> 00:53:01.520
Yep.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:03.560
Ah!

00:53:02.760 --> 00:53:06.160
That's four G now.

00:53:05.360 --> 00:53:07.640
I can't put my hand up now!

00:53:07.000 --> 00:53:14.440
At four G, I'm losing my ability to react.

00:53:15.080 --> 00:53:19.320
And we're way off the pace of Neville Duke.

00:53:19.720 --> 00:53:30.920
There are no crowds on the coast today for us, but when Duke set out on that last record-breaking run, holiday-makers lined the route.

00:53:30.200 --> 00:53:35.440
So, what did Duke feel, flying above those holiday crowds?

00:53:36.240 --> 00:53:37.720
Yes!

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:43.520
Sheer pleasure.

00:53:46.400 --> 00:53:59.840
Back on board my vintage ride, I'm exploring all these shores have to offer for the Great Guide.

00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:02.800
It's clear that many here still feel the need for speed.

00:54:02.000 --> 00:54:09.040
It was on this coast that Britain pioneered powerboat racing.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:17.960
I'm on my way to Poole Harbour, the second-largest natural harbour in the world.

00:54:17.120 --> 00:54:22.240
It's in our Great Guide as THE place to powerboat.

00:54:21.360 --> 00:54:22.520
Hello, Ian.

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:25.920
It's where I'm meeting Ian Toll.

00:54:24.960 --> 00:54:26.240
I'm impressed already.

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:28.840
You're very kind, thank you very much.

00:54:28.000 --> 00:54:32.200
He's taking me for a spin in his 1971 Magnum.

00:54:31.520 --> 00:54:34.760
Can we make some noise for you?

00:54:33.840 --> 00:54:35.000
Please do.

00:54:34.040 --> 00:54:35.720
I'm not sure what to expect.

00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:36.440
Oh, yeah!

00:54:37.760 --> 00:54:39.080
I think it's on(!)

00:54:38.920 --> 00:54:44.360
I'm already terrified, based purely on the noise it's making!

00:54:43.720 --> 00:54:47.680
So things can only get worse.

00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:57.800
Ian's a veteran of what is now the longest-running offshore powerboat race in the world.

00:54:57.040 --> 00:55:06.200
Launched in 1961, the Cowes-Torquay race was the first of its kind in Britain.

00:55:05.440 --> 00:55:12.080
155 miles of pure speed, drawing spectators in their thousands.

00:55:12.680 --> 00:55:23.320
Back on board Magnum, Ian doesn't need any excuse to go full throttle.

00:55:50.160 --> 00:55:59.240
Ian's passion for powerboat racing has become a family affair.

00:55:58.440 --> 00:56:01.680
His son Christian has the speed bug too.

00:56:00.960 --> 00:56:03.880
I've never been in anything like that.

00:56:02.960 --> 00:56:05.880
I think we are doing about 60-something miles...

00:56:05.080 --> 00:56:06.920
60 to 70.

00:56:05.960 --> 00:56:12.920
And, obviously, I've travelled at that speed in a car on innumerable occasions, but on the water it felt like 260mph.

00:56:11.960 --> 00:56:13.120
Absolutely.

00:56:12.360 --> 00:56:15.880
It's the rain in your face.

00:56:15.000 --> 00:56:16.240
Absolutely.

00:56:15.400 --> 00:56:18.440
It's like being tattooed by God.

00:56:17.600 --> 00:56:21.160
Tell me how you got involved with the sport.

00:56:20.200 --> 00:56:22.720
It's my father's fault, you see.

00:56:21.920 --> 00:56:30.920
He started all of the madness in 1968 and bought a very famous powerboat that sank in a very famous powerboat race.

00:56:30.200 --> 00:56:32.840
So tell me about that.

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:33.760
What's the story there?

00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:41.520
It was a world champion powerboat that came over for the 1968 Cowes-Torquay.

00:56:40.680 --> 00:56:43.920
And it had the best of everything.

00:56:42.960 --> 00:56:47.760
Unfortunately, it sank and went down in 160 feet of water.

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:48.160
And I bought it on the bottom.

00:56:47.240 --> 00:56:48.680
And that was the start.

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:49.440
You can do that?

00:56:48.480 --> 00:56:50.280
Oh, yes.

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:50.640
I bought the wreck.

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:58.200
That boat was a Magnum Tornado, at the time, the most famous powerboat in the world.

00:56:57.240 --> 00:57:09.720
Salvaged from the seabed, Ian rebuilt and completely restored it - eventually selling it to buy a house.

00:57:08.760 --> 00:57:14.600
Since then, father and son have been obsessively restoring and racing vintage powerboats on this coast.

00:57:13.760 --> 00:57:17.640
Shall we say we have an addiction with these boats?

00:57:16.680 --> 00:57:20.240
What is this one, and why is it so conspicuously long?

00:57:19.360 --> 00:57:34.080
OK, so as the evolution of offshore powerboats came along, they got longer and longer, and the idea of having a longer boat is the bow really protects you, it absorbs the energy of the wave, and you can drive it in harder seas.

00:57:33.120 --> 00:57:36.160
So you can put more horsepower in a longer boat.

00:57:35.320 --> 00:57:39.400
This boat has about 1,400 horsepower.

00:57:38.640 --> 00:57:41.880
It's another animal again.

00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:43.080
"Animal" is the word.

00:57:42.400 --> 00:57:44.800
Yeah.

00:57:44.040 --> 00:57:45.320
And it really is exhilarating.

00:57:44.440 --> 00:57:45.760
Get it trimmed right, and the boat's flying.

00:57:45.080 --> 00:57:46.360
It's just second to none, it really is.

00:58:00.440 --> 00:58:08.880
At the heart of England's south coast is a shoreline awash with your pick of pleasures.

00:58:08.160 --> 00:58:17.240
Around every twist and turn, ancient history rubs shoulders with the cutting edge.

00:58:16.440 --> 00:58:25.160
Coastal competitors spur each other on to bigger, better, faster.

00:58:24.480 --> 00:58:34.280
Touring with our Great Guide, at Portsmouth we saw the future of yachting.

00:58:33.400 --> 00:58:37.400
We scaled the famous Needles on the Isle of Wight.

00:58:36.640 --> 00:58:41.240
On the Jurassic Coast, we saw secrets hidden in the rock.

00:58:40.400 --> 00:58:44.080
And we sailed in style on the Solent.

00:58:43.120 --> 00:58:45.920
This is our Great Guide.

00:58:45.040 --> 00:58:46.800
Come and see it for yourself.

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:47.240
Let's go!

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:48.000
He's a scientist.

00:58:47.160 --> 00:58:48.480
Brilliant, apparently.

00:58:56.880 --> 00:58:58.040
Get him to work for us.

00:58:58.200 --> 00:58:59.320
Doesn't matter how I do it.

00:58:59.040 --> 00:59:00.440
You may be bringing people over here who did things during the war.

00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:00.680
I will not work for you.

00:58:59.800 --> 00:59:01.240
I will not work for the British Government.

00:59:24.200 --> 00:59:25.280
Let us not let the past haunt all of our actions.

00:59:24.480 --> 00:59:25.680
You've got to do something!

00:59:28.520 --> 00:59:29.640
It's only you that can!

00:59:39.800 --> 00:59:40.840
We are people of talent, people of vision, people of passion.

00:59:58.080 --> 00:59:59.280
..Actually dare to be prime minister.

00:59:59.640 --> 01:00:00.720
The new generation who are going to unveil the plaque to Bill Richmond.

00:59:59.840 --> 01:00:01.360
From a black historical point of view, it's monumental.

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:01.800
If you complained about me being black, that's who I am, a black woman, caring for you.

01:00:00.840 --> 01:00:03.640
Have you come to shake a leg?

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:04.040
No, no, I can't...

01:00:03.080 --> 01:00:04.560
Surely!

01:00:03.600 --> 01:00:04.920
Every chant, and every racial chant, you definitely heard.

01:00:04.000 --> 01:00:05.160
But my team-mates were laughing.

01:00:05.480 --> 01:00:06.680
We are people of talent, people of vision, people of passion.

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:07.000
We have a contribution to make.

01:00:06.120 --> 01:00:07.320
A landmark season of programmes...

01:00:06.360 --> 01:00:07.520
'You've tried everything.'

01:00:06.560 --> 01:00:08.720
Does it hurt you to touch the control?

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:10.520
Yeah.

01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:11.040
'There's nowhere else to turn.'

01:00:10.400 --> 01:00:11.840
To sign my name would be an incredible thing.

01:00:10.880 --> 01:00:12.320
'Graham needs a better way to communicate.

01:00:13.040 --> 01:00:14.400
'What if the best brains in Britain could help to fix your life?'

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:15.880
My challenge is immense.

01:00:15.080 --> 01:00:16.280
You know you're doing it for the right reasons, so you keep going.

01:00:16.320 --> 01:00:17.400
Gah!

01:00:16.680 --> 01:00:17.920
Did it just work?

01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:22.800
It's life-changing.

01:00:21.840 --> 01:00:23.480
I can't thank you enough.
